WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.800
Good morning, John.

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.200
For the Project for Awesome last year, Travis McElroy and I did a one-off podcast called the Dadcast,

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:10.560
a podcast in which two brand new dads answered questions about being new dads,

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:15.600
and the most common question we got was, "how do you decide?"

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.660
How do you make this tremendous... tremendous decision?

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:22.860
So, I'm going to start this video off by answering the question.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:24.400
You don't know!

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.720
It's like deciding to buy 378 bottles of olives if you've never had an olive.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:33.620
You just won't know what a terrible, awful, poisonous, turd fruit they are until you put one in your mouth.

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:39.700
Or, alternately, your child could be the finest piece of tree flesh you've ever consumed...

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:41.060
This metaphor fell apart.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:44.120
Either way, you've got 378 jars of these things...

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:45.900
except they're a human being!

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:50.080
I was never really opposed or enthusiastic about the idea of having a kid.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:51.760
Here's the crazy thing, though. After six months,

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.420
I can tell you that my pros and cons list was wrong!

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:58.920
Here's some stuff that I should have had on my cons list, like the worst parts of parenthood.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.340
It's not the puking, and the pooping,

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:03.320
and the getting up in the middle of the night, and never sleeping a normal amount again.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.960
All of those things actually are surprisingly easy.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:10.840
Sometimes I even like being excessively needed by this adorable biscuit man.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:11.720
What I don't like:

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:17.160
1. The stress of knowing that my child could get ill at any moment, seriously,

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:19.600
or just generally, something terrible might happen,

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.900
and while you can minimize risk, you cannot eliminate it.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:24.040
2. Feeling like I'm doing it wrong.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.640
3. Feeling like I can't do it at all,

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.620
and when you have a human being who's depending on you for everything,

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:34.220
feeling like you can't do it is a feeling of tremendous failure,

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:36.840
even when it's fleeting, even when you know it's not true.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:41.500
I think everybody experiences this, and it's just one of those "it gets better" situations.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:46.740
4, and this is kind of wrapped up in all of these: caring so much that it hurts.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:49.520
I was also wrong about the reasons that I would like having a kid,

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:53.300
like, I knew I was going to like having, like, a little Katherine–Hank hybrid person,

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:57.700
and that's nice, but here are the bigger and unexpected ones.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:03.400
1. Embarking on a really big, new, interesting project with my wife, who's my favorite person.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.740
2. I get to sing wherever I want to.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:08.680
As long as he's with me, people will think it's cute and not weird.

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:12.140
3. I think I have a much better understanding of my parents' relationship with me,

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:16.140
which is maybe hard to have, unless you experience parenthood yourself.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:21.960
4. I have a much better understanding of, and am very impressed by the capabilities of both me and Katherine.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:25.740
5. I really like doing a thing that is super normal,

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:31.000
like ev— lots of people do this, but it's also magnificently important, and I know that.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:36.340
Like, every piece of me unquestionably knows that being a dad is vitally important.

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:38.600
6. I love to look at him,

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.860
which I didn't really expect. I love it so much that,

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:46.780
I like, show him to other people and I'm like, "I'm so sorry that you don't get to stare at this all the time like I do."

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:51.180
Finally, 7. Caring so much it hurts.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:53.500
Again, this video doesn't have any answers.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:56.980
It is just the perspective of one of billions of parents on Earth.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:59.640
It is hard! I will tell you that, but it—

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:02.760
also, the hard things are the ones that are the most worth doing,

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:06.580
and that's not an endorsement of having a kid, it is an endorsement of doing hard things.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:09.600
You are the only person who can decide whether you're ready to create a human,

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.720
but, final thing, if you are like me and you assume that all the people

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.100
who are talking about how great parenthood is were just making it up so that they would feel less bad

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:19.800
about the terrible decision they made, it's not made up.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.320
It's actually really great.

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:22.880
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:23.940
End screen! Two things.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:27.900
First, the Kedicizdim shirts and stuff will only be available for another couple days,

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:30.400
including: we made a laptop decal,

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:33.240
because people were talking in the comments about how much they wanted a laptop decal;

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:36.480
and two, John, you and I are going to be in Seattle, December 9 and 10 for PodCon,

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.360
a convention for people who love and make podcasts.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.579
We're doing it in partnership with 
 the Night Vale Presents team

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:45.220
and those good, good McElroy boys of My Brother, My Brother, and Me.

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:47.920
PodCon.com—we're doing a crowdfunding campaign right now

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:50.320
to try and raise more money to get more cool guests;

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:51.800
we just announced that Criminal is gonna be there.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:53.500
For more information, PodCon.com.

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:55.980
There's also a bunch of social medias you can follow in the description.

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:59.120
It's gonna be a good time!

